To access the fourth element of a linked list in Java, you need to traverse the list starting from the head node. Since linked lists do not support random access like arrays, you must iterate through the nodes one by one until you reach the fourth one.

Here is a simple example using a custom `Node` class and a `LinkedList` class:

```java
class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;

    Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}

public class LinkedList {
    private Node head;

    // Method to add a node at the end (for demonstration)
    public void add(int data) {
        Node newNode = new Node(data);
        if (head == null) {
            head = newNode;
            return;
        }
        Node current = head;
        while (current.next != null) {
            current = current.next;
        }
        current.next = newNode;
    }

    // Method to get the fourth element
    public int getFourthElement() {
        if (head == null) {
            throw new NoSuchElementException("List is empty");
        }

        Node current = head;
        int count = 0;

        while (current != null) {
            count++;
            if (count == 4) {
                return current.data;
            }
            current = current.next;
        }

        throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException("Fourth element does not exist");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinkedList list = new LinkedList();
        list.add(10);
        list.add(20);
        list.add(30);
        list.add(40);
        list.add(50);

        try {
            int fourth = list.getFourthElement();
            System.out.println("The fourth element is: " + fourth);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

